finding: Candidate 1507a284: the cited measurement does not establish the claimed household-dust albedo. Linke et al. (2006), https://acp.copernicus.org/articles/6/3315/2006/, measured resuspended Saharan mineral dust; its 0.98–0.99 at 532 nm is accurately quoted, but neither that material nor a single green wavelength establishes a wavelength-independent SSA for indoor household motes under the room's lighting. Household dust is a heterogeneous mixture (see https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8582694/), not established here as those mineral samples. 0.98 is physically admissible and may be a reasonable explicit mineral-like artistic assumption; presenting it as a measured justification for this room's dust leaves the rider's source-fit claim unproved. Qualify the material/model assumption or supply evidence applicable to it; no particular replacement number follows from this finding. Independently examined: complete candidate diff and commit message, unit stage 5, motes shader and additive blend, public readFogInScatter contract and implementation, external source scope, and all three particles Node files by execution. The scalar is indeed isolated as albedo in the example's stated approximation: coverage alpha, camera fade, quad size, exposed local in-scatter and camera-path transmittance are separate; additive blending introduces no hidden alpha multiplier. Only the constant and its two explanatory comment lines changed. All 10 Node tests passed on real Apple M4 Max; they verify rendering behavior, not whether the optical material citation fits household dust. No files edited or landed. Remaining uncertainty: no household-dust SSA measurement or spectral/material model was supplied; no independent browser/visual assessment performed.
